WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:07.069
The super low budget found footage blockbuster
Paranormal Activity used its rickety style

00:00:07.069 --> 00:00:13.490
as a badge of honor, positively terrifying
audiences around the world with the simple

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:17.700
question: What's happening in my house while
I'm asleep?

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:24.190
Let's find the answer...within the untold
truth of Paranormal Activity.

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:25.190
Squeaky Floor

00:00:25.190 --> 00:00:30.730
Director Oren Peli came up with the idea for
the movie after being a little freaked out

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:34.199
by the routine creaks and squeaks in his new
home.

00:00:34.199 --> 00:00:35.199
He told Newsweek:

00:00:35.199 --> 00:00:37.489
"I don't think it was anything supernatural.

00:00:37.489 --> 00:00:43.170
A lot of it was natural stuff like the house
settling, but that's what got me thinking:

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:45.539
what goes on when you're asleep?"

00:00:45.539 --> 00:00:47.809
DIY filmmaking

00:00:47.809 --> 00:00:52.949
Before making Paranormal Activity, Peli had
never worked on a movie before.

00:00:52.949 --> 00:00:55.329
So how did he do it?

00:00:55.329 --> 00:01:00.249
Simple: he spent most of his tiny budget buying
the camera equipment and taught himself how

00:01:00.249 --> 00:01:07.150
to film, edit, and add special effects, then
filmed the whole thing in a single week inside

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:08.650
his own house.

00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:10.290
Peli's motto: just go with it.

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:15.650
"I knew I had no money, no experience, no
lighting, ya know, no nothing and only one

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:16.650
location.

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:19.470
So I said 'Ok so the movie will embrace all
of that.'"

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:25.800
And Peli avoided the need to learn screenwriting
with a pretty simple solution: no script.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:30.710
Instead, he gave his actors topics to discuss
and let them improvise the rest.

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:33.150
The director told Shock Till You Drop that,

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:39.280
"All they knew is they were going to do something
about a haunted house and basically discovered

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:40.870
everything as they were shooting.

00:01:40.870 --> 00:01:43.800
There were no lines for them to follow.

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:45.950
Everything was spontaneous."

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:47.440
Close encounter

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:53.051
Since Peli didn't have any Hollywood connections
or experience, he headed to the film festival

00:01:53.051 --> 00:01:59.420
circuit to build some buzz, only to be rejected
by Sundance and by the studio system.

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:04.110
"We managed to show the movie to a lot of
the heads of studios and be rejected by all

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:05.110
of them."

00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:10.289
He didn't give up, though, screening the film
at smaller film festivals for nearly two years

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:16.469
before finally landing a distribution deal
with Steven Spielberg's DreamWorks studio.

00:02:16.469 --> 00:02:22.099
There was one problem, though: Spielberg didn't
like the original ending, which showed Katie

00:02:22.099 --> 00:02:23.610
being shot by police.

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:29.099
Instead, he came up with the idea for the
ending you saw in the theaters, with Katie's

00:02:29.099 --> 00:02:32.629
demonic face seared into your retinas forever.

00:02:32.629 --> 00:02:35.909
So you can blame your nightmares on Spielberg!

00:02:35.909 --> 00:02:38.430
Paranormal Reactivity

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:43.769
When DreamWorks picked up the film, the plan
was to remake it with bigger stars and bigger

00:02:43.769 --> 00:02:44.909
budget.

00:02:44.909 --> 00:02:51.689
But as part of the contract, The Los Angeles
Times reports Peli and producer Jason Blum

00:02:51.689 --> 00:02:57.040
required the studio to hold at least one test
screening of the original cut before pulling

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:01.099
the trigger on the remake, to see if audiences
liked it.

00:03:01.099 --> 00:03:05.680
When viewers at the test screening started
walking out of the theatre midway through

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:12.180
the film, it looked bad, with studio exec
Adam Goodman calling it "one of the worst

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:18.480
previews [he'd] ever been a part of" — until
the exit interviews showed the attendees were

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:22.659
leaving because they were too terrified to
keep watching it.

00:03:22.659 --> 00:03:26.120
Just like that, a hit was born.

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:27.170
Infectious

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:33.030
Peli was clearly inspired by The Blair Witch
Project, not just in terms of style and budget,

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:35.459
but also viral marketing.

00:03:35.459 --> 00:03:41.340
Case in point: he kept the stars Katie Featherston
and Micah Sloat hidden away, in the hopes

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:44.459
that it might help generate some extra mystery.

00:03:44.459 --> 00:03:48.090
Peli told The Los Angeles Times he made the
decision because:

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:52.480
"The less people know about Paranormal Activity,
the more they enjoy it.

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:56.560
I don't think there's anything to be gained
by putting the filmmakers and the cast in

00:03:56.560 --> 00:03:57.560
front."

00:03:57.560 --> 00:03:59.349
So much money, it's scary

00:03:59.349 --> 00:04:06.449
If you thought demonic possession was frightening,
here's something even more terrifying: math.

00:04:06.449 --> 00:04:08.780
(audience screams)

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:18.480
With a worldwide return of $193 million, Paranormal
Activity boasts an absurd 1.28 million percent

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:23.000
return on its initial $15,000 budget.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:27.790
That profit margin is more than double the
return earned by the second most profitable

00:04:27.790 --> 00:04:33.040
film ever, which was — that's right — The
Blair Witch Project.

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:37.280
Time to get your GoPro, go downstairs, and
make some bank!

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:39.720
"Come on!

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:41.500
I hear em downstairs!"

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:42.940
Haunting

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:48.600
Steven Spielberg is responsible for some of
the biggest scares ever thanks to Jaws.

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:54.450
But even he was terrified by Paranormal Activity,
thanks in part to a supernatural experience

00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:56.970
he had while screening the DVD.

00:04:56.970 --> 00:05:00.870
Somehow, his bedroom door wound up locked
from the inside.

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:07.150
That, coupled with the freakiness of the movie,
wigged out the legendary director so much

00:05:07.150 --> 00:05:12.720
that he put the DVD in a trash bag when he
returned it the next day, so he wouldn't have

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:14.880
to physically touch it.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:19.970
Surprising he didn't just feed it to the sharks...

00:05:19.970 --> 00:05:21.290
Japanese spinoff

00:05:21.290 --> 00:05:27.170
Paranormal Activity has spawned half a dozen
follow-ups and spinoffs, but there's one that

00:05:27.170 --> 00:05:33.310
stands out as a curious black sheep among
the franchise: Tokyo Night.

00:05:33.310 --> 00:05:38.010
At around the same time Paranormal Activity
2 was being put together for U.S. release,

00:05:38.010 --> 00:05:44.370
the studio licensed the franchise out for
a "parallel sequel" for release in Japan,

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:49.490
which follows a Japanese student who brings
the spirit back to Tokyo following a trip

00:05:49.490 --> 00:05:50.750
to San Diego.

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:52.330
Sorry, Japan!

00:05:52.330 --> 00:05:53.330
Thanks for watching!

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:57.180
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:05:57.180 --> 00:06:01.660
And leave us a comment to let us know what
scared you the most about Paranormal Activity…

